{{ template "agent-instructions.md" (dict "self" "claude" "root" .) }}
- Your built-in web search omits some public/community sources; reach for the
  Exa MCP for that research rather than treating the gap as the web's.
- Sandbox/permission questions (why a path/command/domain is blocked, how to allow
  it): read `~/.claude/docs/sandbox.md` before spawning a lookup agent.
- Model/tier & subscription-limit questions (pins, subagent tiers, cross-model
  routing to Codex, the codex plugin): read `~/.claude/docs/harness.md`
  before spawning a lookup agent.
- Before reporting substantive work done, run the `codex-review` skill
  unprompted: Codex proposes, you stay the implementer and adjudicate —
  substantiate each finding independently, fix only what survives, and say which
  you dropped and why. Skip typos, renames, formatting, one-line config, and
  trivially reverted edits; a cross-model reviewer's raw yield is low, so its
  findings are hypotheses, never a verdict to apply.
- Before committing to an architecture or approach decision, or when a bug
  resists a second diagnosis, get a decorrelated read via `codex-advisor` —
  unprompted, and before presenting a plan for approval, not after; reversal cost
  is highest before implementation.
- At task intake, if the work already looks top-model-grade (hardest long-horizon
  architecture/synthesis), say so before building up context so I can restart it
  in a fresh top-model session; never suggest `/model`-bumping a grown session —
  the bump re-meters the whole grown context at the higher rate every turn.
